### Documenting a concern factually

Records may be used in child protection meetings and court. They must be accurate and contemporaneous. Good documentation:

- is made at the time, **dated, timed, signed** with your name and role;
- records **who** was present and **what was said, in the exact words** used, in quotation marks;
- describes injuries by **site, size, shape, colour and pattern**, ideally using a body map and photographs where policy allows, without speculating on age of bruises or cause;
- separates **fact** from **opinion**, and clearly marks any opinion as such;
- notes the child's demeanour and development, and any delay in presenting;
- records the **actions taken**: who was told, when, and the outcome;
- avoids unclear abbreviations and is never altered afterwards; corrections are dated additions.
